A||N||21||bHooper||pricing a 3.4 liter..||||1||
Z||000000||bHooper||06-07-2000||09:47 AM||||I have found a possible source for a 3.4 liter.  here is ther response to my enquiry.  Tell me what you think.  Is this a good deal?<P>"The engines come out of 93-95 Firebirds and Camaros.   The motors are $400 and are complete long blocks w/intake & Injectors, water pump and balancer. They do not come with a  flywheel and do not have distributors. They all include 30 day warranties.  I have 2 left, both in the 60K mile area.<P>If you are doing a conversion.  An 89 2.8, 90-92 3.1, or 93-95 3.4 flywheel is required, 84-88 2.8 flywheels are not balanced correctly.  The 3.4's do not have a fuel pump boss, so an electric fuel pump is required.  <BR>The 3.4's use DIS (No distributor), but any 2.8/3.1 TBI/MPFI intake will bolt on and you can install a distributor, by removing the oil pump drive plug.  All exhaust manifolds fit.  All 3.4's are roller cam motors."<P>Is this the right way to go?<P>thanks,<BR>hoop<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||jlruiz67||06-07-2000||11:51 AM||||Sounds great to me. Do you think you could tell us where your are getting this from so I could get in it later when I can save some $$$$$ and start my project. My 2.8 V6 has crossed the 200,000 mark and I would like to run a 3.4 after summer. Its great to know that my V6 has 200K miles and still running strong, but I know that the time has come for an upgrade. Thanks for any info.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||06-07-2000||01:29 PM||||My Gt only has 110,600 miles on it.  But the car sat after bening broken, for 4 years.  I put a new tranny in it, dut apparently did a pretty lame job of replacing the rear main oil seal... it is weaping!<P>I had planned on doing a swap, within the next year or so.  But am thinking of excelerating that swap, now.<P>I am debating purchasing a engine with that many miles on it, when I can get one with less than 20,000 for around $800.  But then agian, I have just started searching... haven't even visted any of the local yards!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||06-07-2000||07:46 PM||||My 3.4 originally costed $450, but after I had a talk with the guy about the weird smell when I pulled the oil pan, he dropped the price to $250. I can tell you this- my motor came out of a 95 Camaro and it is NOT a roller cam engine. However, performance is very VERY good! If Pontiac had made them have this much power, they would still be selling them.||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||06-07-2000||10:40 PM||||so what do they mean by "roller cam"?  is that the TDC engine, or the pushrod?<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000005||pollock||06-08-2000||09:27 AM||||The pushrod 3.4 that was in camaros, other cars, and the HT3.4 crate motor, IS NOT a roller cam motor.  According to what i've read, NO 60 DEGREE PUSHROD GM V6 WAS EVER AVAILABLE AS A ROLLER CAM MOTOR.<P>There is a roller cam available from GM to  go in the 60* V6, but i think you'll have to fabricate your own lifter retainer bracket.  <P>The 3.4 TDC (aka, DOHC) probably has roller tips on the cam followers, but i'm not sure.  Whole different ball game to put that in a fiero anyway.||||reg||4
Z||000006||bHooper||06-08-2000||10:29 AM||||Huh?<P>Is it a blue or a yellow lego?<P>I am not following your explination... it sounds like you are telling me that this engine will not work in the fiero.  I thought all '93-95 Camero/Firebird 3.4 liters were the correct pushrod 6 cylinder engines for our cars??<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||pollock||06-08-2000||02:27 PM||||Didn't mean to confuse ya.<P>The Camaro motor will work fine.  But it does not have a roller cam.  I guess that's all i was trying to say.<P>Lego?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula||06-08-2000||03:25 PM||||The only reason I didnt buy a 3.4 is because it says on v8 archies site that you have to cut the block for an automatic.....  Would that be the tdc or the regular or the extra value meal?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||06-08-2000||05:50 PM||||Spoke with Ed Parks (the Fiero Factory/3.4 guru) yesterday about this.  Yes, the 3.4 does have to be cut for the auto. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  In case you're mechanically challenged like me, Ed does a less-than-40k-miles 3.4 for $2500 and a brand new/reman 3.5/3.6 for $3300.  BTW-there are some other mods that you have to do, too such as redrilling for the starter, etc.<P>pollock--TDC is Twin Double overhead Cams.  Different from the Double Over Head Cams.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Tom88gt||06-09-2000||08:37 AM||||Maybe no roller cam.  But roller tip rocker arms have been available for years.  I think Magnum Cams makes them.  PAW or Summit would be a good source.<P>3.5/3.6?  What is that?  A bored and/or stroked 3.4?  How much more can the 2.8/3.1/3.4 grow before it self-destructs?||||reg||4
Z||000011||BBBAD GT||06-09-2000||09:40 AM||||I'll try and clear up some confusing between roller cams and flat tappet cams.  A roller cam has lifters (that roll on the cam) with wheels on the ends of it.  A flat tappet cam has a lifter that doesn't have a roller on it, and its just a flat piece of metal that rolls on the cam.  With a flat tappet cam, you can not get too agressive with the ramp rates, or else you can technically cut into the lifter wrong.  With a roller cam, you can get pretty agressive with the ramp rates with the same lift of a similar flat tappet cam.  This is the way I understand it.  A diagram would be better, but I can't find one on the net, but I did find this great example of worn flat tappet lifters from Crane:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://cranecams.com/instructions/valvetrain/dlifters.jpg">  <P><BR>Oh, I forgot to mention, the lifter on a flat tappet cam can move around at will in its lifter bore, where as a roller lifter has to be positioned just in the right place and a retainer of some sort to keep it there (thats the trick to converting any block to roller block).  Just think if the roller lifter was free to move around in the bore, it wouldn't be pretty.  <BR> <p>[This message has been edited by BBBAD GT (edited 06-09-2000).]||||reg||7
Z||000012||pollock||06-09-2000||09:41 AM||||Technically, Archie's right, but that makes it sound worse than it really is.  I've seen some pictures of the necessary "cut" for an automatic, and it's really just grinding a little off one corner of the block.<P>Monkey, TDC=DOHC, when the engine in question has 2 banks of cylinders, i.e., v8 or v6.  Otherwise, why would engines with just one cam on top of each head be called "SOHC".  Probably the best way to say it is "4 cam" or "2 cam", or 24 valve, or 12 valve, etc.<P>A .030-over 3.4 would probably be about a 3.5.  Don't sweat it though.... engines that were originally 3.4s had thicker walls, and reputedly can be bored more.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||06-09-2000||10:27 AM||||I haven't done the math (and don't really want to <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">), but I think a .030 overbore is a 3.5 and a .060 overbore is a 3.6?  All I know is that Ed Parks said that he gets 3.5 and 3.6L remanufactured motors all the time for his swaps.<P>As far as the TDC/DOHC thing, I thought we had this discussion a while back and came up with my answer.  Doesn't really matter as far as I'm concerned, though.  As long as the thing runs and is reliable (and can take Vettes off the line <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">), it makes no difference how many cams it's got. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||pollock||06-09-2000||04:27 PM||||I'll drink to that.<P>Sorry to have gotten off topic.||||reg||14
Z||000015||bHooper||06-12-2000||01:45 PM||||i got a funny email, from that engine supplier, this weekend.  it went something like: "camero 3.4's are rwd, a fiero is setup like a fwd... you cant use a rwd engine in a fwd setup."  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>i responded with something like: "an engine is a engine, merely a means of propulsion... a tranny is what seperates fwd from rwd"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>today i got this response: "FWD engines do not have conventional mounting systems for engine cradles.  FWD engines use "Short" accessories and are mounted sideways.  The blocks are also not the same.  It's not just a matter of rotating the engine 90 degrees and saying it's a FWD engine now.  Used 3.4's out of 93-95 Camaros are $400."<P>imagination, is what seperates the can's from the can'ts!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000016||Old Lar||06-12-2000||01:56 PM||||Hoop:  Let us all know how sucessful you are with the conversion of the RWD engine from a Camaro to the Fiero.<P>Everything I've looked at is that it is easier said than done, and that a lot of rework is required on the cradle, engine mounts, starter locations, water pump pullys, exhaust manifolds, and wireing to get to work correctly.<P>It may be an engine combination that the assembler can figure out, but the next owner won't have a clue.||||reg||6
Z||000017||bHooper||06-12-2000||02:43 PM||||Old Lar, I don't believe I that I will be rushing into the job, i.e.: it may be this winter b4 I actually start working on it.<P>As for the next owner, my car is a 2 owner car... and I have had it since 1989!  Reckon I'll keep it unless something big happens!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Besides, I have not ruled out letting Ed Parks do the actual work.||||reg||1
Z||000018||BN Boomer||06-12-2000||03:04 PM||||Next owner? What the heck is that? Are saying that you are planning on selling your Fieros or something?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>My understanding about the 3.4L as found in RWD Camaros or available as a crate engine directly from GM is that it is nearly a bolt in affair and is probably the simplest engine swap/upgrade of all. The 3.4 is just a slightly revised 60 degree V6 that has been poked and stroked to up the displacement. All that needs to be done to install it into a Fiero is to have the holes for the starter mounting bolts drilled and threaded on the proper side and, if you plan on using it with an automatic transmission, the block itself needs to be be trimmed up a bit in back to fit properly mate to the TH125. The engine mounting points are in the same location and the intake system, exhaust manifolds, and all accessories from the 2.8 will bolt right on. I'm going to be looking into this particular swap a lot more next year and I'm also interested in adding a 4 speed automatic at the same time.<P>If you've ever seen a 2.8L Fiero engine with all of the mounting hardware and accessories removed, it looks just like the RWD engine out of an 80s Camaro or S-10 pickup.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||87PegasusGT||06-12-2000||03:05 PM||||What do you think I have? I didn't use no truck engine. They are full of !@#$. After you redrill the starter holes, its a piece of cake. Just get the engine and don't listen to them.I have a 96 Camaro 3.4. (Hm... I didn't know those were FWD)||||reg||1
Z||000020||batboy||06-12-2000||03:34 PM||||Remember, Cooter just did this swap, so he should be able to answer most of your concerns. I think the engine provider is just using the standard cautioning statement. They don't want you buying this engine thinking it's a direct replacement (because it's not). Like was mentioned already, any swap is going to be easier said than done, but of the possible swaps involving bigger and more powerful engines, the 3.4 pushrod conversion is probably the easiest (except maybe the 3.1). Ed Parks has this swap down to a science, but it will still be a lot of work if you're planning to do it yourself.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 06-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||Old Lar||06-12-2000||09:07 PM||||My sentiments exactly.  I'm letting an expert do my transplant.  Bob Steiger has my new 3.4 in a crate in his garage.  Since he's also switching the transmission, there are many modifications that need to be done.<P>And he has his chrome back for his Zimmer.||||reg||6
